Equate the coefficients of $x^{n-1}$ in $(1+x)^n(1+x)^n=(1+x)^{2n}$:
$$\begin{align*}
\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\binom nj\binom n{n-1-j}
&=\binom{2n}{n-1}.
\end{align*}$$
By symmetry, $\binom n{n-1-j}=\binom n{j+1}$. Therefore
$$\begin{align*}
\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\binom nj\binom n{j+1}
&=\binom{2n}{n-1}\\
\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\binom nj\binom n{j+1}
&=\frac{(2n)!}{(n-1)!(n+1)!}.
\end{align*}$$
This is the required adjacent-index sum.
